# nginx routing example

ASG is normally deployed behind nginx. This example shows the routing shape:
path-based split between the public edge and the gateway's optimization path,
plus the streaming settings that matter (no buffering).

Replace `asg.example.com` with your domain and `1.2.3.4:18788` with your
gateway address.

```nginx
# /etc/nginx/sites-available/asg

# Public edge: everything /v1 goes to the ASG gateway
server {
    listen 80;
    listen [::]:80;
    server_name asg.example.com;

    location /v1/ {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:18788;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;

        # Streaming must not be buffered
        proxy_buffering off;
        proxy_cache off;
        proxy_read_timeout 300s;
        proxy_send_timeout 300s;
    }

    # Health check
    location = /healthz {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:18788/healthz;
        access_log off;
    }
}
```

## Notes

- **Disable buffering** for `/v1/` — SSE streams stall if the proxy buffers.
- **Timeouts** — long streaming generations need read timeouts well above the
  slowest turn (here 300s).
- **TLS** — terminate at nginx (Let's Encrypt) so clients use `https://`.
- The gateway itself binds to localhost; only nginx is exposed.
